from pathlib import Path
import sys,math
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('18');add=A.add
add('4.3.3-01','Divergent rays spread apart, convergent rays approach a meeting point and parallel rays maintain separation.','Each ray is locally straight in a uniform medium between optical interactions.','The beam describes a collection of propagation directions. A single ray has no widening width in the simple model. Follow arrowheads to decide whether a set approaches or leaves a point; the same drawn lines without direction can represent different propagation.','Explain how ray directions distinguish convergent and divergent beams.','Link arrow direction to separation: convergent rays approach one another.~Divergent rays spread farther apart as they travel.')
add('4.3.3-01-M01','Divergent rays spread apart as they propagate.','Their backward extensions may indicate a real source or a virtual image.','Light leaving a small source diverges through space. The growing beam does not mean each ray bends outward continually in uniform air; the rays leave with different directions. Mark outward arrowheads when constructing the beam.','Explain why rays from a small source form a divergent beam.','They leave the source in different directions.~Their separation increases along propagation.')
add('4.3.3-01-M02','Convergent rays approach one another along propagation.','Actual convergence can form a real image.','A converging lens can redirect an incoming beam toward a point. After crossing, the same rays diverge again. Identify whether the crossing is of actual rays or merely backward extensions before calling an image real.','Explain what happens to convergent rays at a real image point.','Actual rays meet at the point.~They then continue past it and can diverge again.')
add('4.3.3-01-M03','Parallel rays travel in the same direction with fixed separation in a uniform medium.','A distant source can provide an approximately parallel bundle across a small optical instrument.','Parallel does not mean horizontal on every page: a tilted parallel bundle is still parallel. A converging lens focuses rays parallel to its principal axis at its principal focus in the thin-lens model.','Explain why a distant object can be approximated as supplying parallel rays to a small lens.','Its distance is much larger than the lens dimensions.~The rays arriving across the lens then differ very little in direction.')
add('4.3.3-02','Refraction is the change of propagation on entering a medium with a different light speed, usually including a direction change at oblique incidence.','At normal incidence the speed and wavelength can change without bending.','Optical density describes a speed-related property, not simply mass density. Light entering typical glass from air slows and bends toward the normal if incidence is oblique. Source frequency remains unchanged across a stationary interface. Distinguish the transmitted refracted ray from the reflected ray that stays in the first medium.','Define refraction of light.','It is the change in light propagation on crossing between media of different light speed, with bending at oblique incidence.')
add('4.3.3-03','A rectangular block with parallel faces can produce a sideways-displaced emergent ray parallel to the incident ray.','A triangular prism generally gives a net angular deviation.','At each face draw its own normal. Air-to-glass transmission bends toward the normal and glass-to-air transmission away when a refracted ray exists. The parallel faces of a rectangular block undo the angular change but leave displacement. Nonparallel prism faces do not undo it in that way.','Explain the difference between rays emerging from a rectangular glass block and a triangular prism in air.','Link the parallel block faces to an emergent ray parallel to the incident ray, displaced sideways.~A prism’s nonparallel faces generally produce net angular deviation.')
add('4.3.3-03-M01','Refraction through a parallel-sided block has two boundary changes.','For the same outside medium, the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray but can be laterally displaced.','Trace the block outline, incidence point and ray directions. Draw normals perpendicular to both faces. The internal ray bends toward the first normal; the exiting ray bends away from the second. Compare the outside directions only after applying both interactions.','Draw an oblique ray through a rectangular glass block in air, labelling normals and the emergent ray.','Draw bending toward the entry normal and away from the exit normal.~Show the emergent ray parallel to the incident direction and sideways displaced.')
A.geometry('4.3.3-03-M01',[dict(points=[[-2,0],[3,0],[3,-2],[-2,-2],[-2,0]],color='black'),dict(points=[[-1,1.732],[0,0],[.7071,-2],[1.7071,-3.732]],arrow=True),dict(points=[[0,1],[0,-1]],style='--',color='grey'),dict(points=[[.7071,-1],[.7071,-3]],style='--',color='grey')],labels=[[-1.8,-1,'Glass'],[-1.5,1.9,'Incident'],[1.5,-3.6,'Emergent']],title='Parallel-sided glass block in air')
add('4.3.3-03-M02','A prism refracts light at its nonparallel faces.','For a typical glass prism in air, the transmitted ray is deviated toward the prism’s base.','Draw normals at both points rather than using one common vertical line. At entry the ray bends toward the local normal; at exit it bends away if it transmits. An internal incidence above the critical angle would instead give total internal reflection, so not every chosen path can leave the intended face.','Draw a ray passing through two sloping faces of a triangular glass prism in air.','Draw refraction toward the entry-face normal, then away from the exit-face normal.~Show a net deviation toward the base and arrowheads along the actual path.')
A.geometry('4.3.3-03-M02',[dict(points=[[-2,0],[2,0],[0,3.464],[-2,0]],color='black'),dict(points=[[-3,1.059],[-1,1.732],[1,1.732],[3,1.059]],arrow=True),dict(points=[[-1.866,2.232],[-.134,1.232]],style='--',color='grey'),dict(points=[[.134,1.232],[1.866,2.232]],style='--',color='grey')],labels=[[-.6,.2,'Glass'],[-3,.7,'Incident'],[2,.7,'Emergent']],title='Prism ray: symmetric transmitted path')
add('4.3.3-04','A refraction angle is measured from the transmitted ray to the normal in that ray’s medium.','At the second face, construct a new normal at the new incidence point.','Mark two points on each visible ray, remove the block and join the marks to its traced boundary. Join the internal entry and exit points. Use a protractor centred on the interface point and aligned with the normal. Measure acute ray-normal angles, not angles from the surface.','Describe how to measure the refraction angle inside a rectangular block.','Trace the internal ray between entry and exit points, draw the entry normal and measure the angle between that normal and the internal ray.')
add('4.3.3-05','For air-to-material transmission, relative refractive index is n = sin i/sin r.','Both angles are measured from the normal using degree-mode trigonometry when angles are in degrees.','If i = 30° and r ≈ 19.5°, n is approximately 1.50. Do not divide the angles themselves; the relation uses their sines. A plot of sin i vertically against sin r horizontally has gradient n. Swapping these axes gives 1/n.','Calculate refractive index when sin i = 0.60 and sin r = 0.40.','n = sin i/sin r = 0.60/0.40.~The refractive index is 1.5.')
add('4.3.3-06','Critical angle is the incidence angle in the optically denser medium for which the refracted ray lies along the boundary.','The refraction angle is then 90° to the normal in the less optically dense medium.','The definition concerns light going toward a lower-index medium, such as glass to air. Above this incidence angle, no propagating refracted ray leaves the surface in the school model and total internal reflection occurs. It is not the angle from the surface and not an air-to-glass threshold.','Define critical angle.','It is the incidence angle in the higher-index medium for which refraction into the lower-index medium is at 90° to the normal.')
add('4.3.3-07','Internal reflection can return part of incident light inside a medium.','Total internal reflection requires travel toward a lower-index medium and incidence above the critical angle.','Below the threshold, both reflected and refracted rays can exist. At the threshold the refracted ray runs along the boundary. Above it the incident light is internally reflected in the ideal model. Optical fibres and suitable prisms can use this effect to guide or turn light without a metal mirror coating.','Explain the conditions for total internal reflection.','Light must approach a lower-index medium from a higher-index medium.~Its incidence angle in that medium must exceed the critical angle.')
add('4.3.3-08','Absolute refractive index is vacuum light speed divided by light speed in the material.','For the same frequency, it is also vacuum wavelength divided by material wavelength.','Frequency remains fixed at a stationary interface, so c = fλ links reduced speed with reduced wavelength. For vacuum speed 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s and material speed 2.0 × 10⁸ m/s, n = 1.5. The index has no unit because it is a ratio of matching quantities.','Calculate refractive index for light speed 2.0 × 10⁸ m/s in a material, taking vacuum speed as 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s.','n = c0/v = (3.0 × 10⁸)/(2.0 × 10⁸).~The index is 1.5.')
add('4.3.3-09','A thin converging lens brings an axis-parallel beam toward its principal focus.','The simple construction applies to suitable near-axis rays.','The lens is thicker at its centre in the usual glass-in-air form. A ray through the optical centre is treated as undeviated in the thin-lens approximation. Real lenses have finite apertures and imperfections, so the ideal point is a model. Diverging lenses are mentioned later for sight correction without a separate image-calculation treatment.','State what an ideal thin converging lens does to rays parallel to its principal axis.','It brings them to its principal focus on the far side.')
add('4.3.3-10','A converging lens’s principal focus is the point where axis-parallel incident rays converge after refraction.','Focal length is the distance from the lens’s optical centre to that point.','A lens has a principal focus on each side for propagation from the corresponding direction. Label the principal axis and the relevant far-side focus before drawing a parallel ray. A focus is a property of the lens model; an object’s image is not necessarily at the focus.','Define the principal focus of a converging lens.','It is the point where rays initially parallel to the principal axis meet after passing through the lens.')
add('4.3.3-11','A distant object gives nearly parallel incident rays across a small lens.','Its sharp real image forms approximately one focal length behind a converging lens.','Use a distant building or tree, never the Sun. Move a screen behind the lens until a sharp image appears, then measure optical-centre-to-screen distance. Repeat with another distant object or position and estimate uncertainty. The approximation improves when object distance greatly exceeds focal length.','Describe how to estimate a converging lens’s focal length using a distant object.','Focus a distant nonsolar object sharply onto a screen and measure the distance from lens centre to screen.')
add('4.3.3-12','A converging lens can form a real image for an object beyond its focus and a virtual image for an object inside it.','Principal rays locate the image by actual intersection or backward extension.','Draw one ray from the object top parallel to the axis, refracted through the far focus. Draw another through the optical centre, undeviated in the thin-lens model. When they meet on the far side the image is real; when outgoing rays diverge, extend them backward to locate a virtual image on the object side.','Explain using a ray diagram how a converging lens forms a real image for an object beyond its focal point.','Link object position to outgoing rays: beyond the focus they can meet to give a real image.~Inside the focus they diverge and backward extensions give a virtual image.')
add('4.3.3-12-M01','For an object beyond a converging lens’s focal point, actual rays can form an inverted real image.','The image may be reduced, equal-sized or enlarged depending on object distance.','With focal length 2 cm and an object 6 cm away, a principal-ray construction gives an image 3 cm on the far side, half as tall and inverted. These values are a diagram stimulus, not a requirement to derive an extra lens equation. Position the screen where actual rays meet.','Draw the real image for a 1 cm tall object 6 cm before a converging lens with focal length 2 cm.','Draw the parallel-then-focus ray and the undeviated centre ray.~Their intersection is 3 cm beyond the lens, 0.5 cm below the axis: a reduced inverted real image.')
def lensdiagram(key,u,v,h,title):
 f=2;hi=-v*h/u
 seg=[dict(points=[[-max(u+1,4),0],[max(v+1,4),0]],color='grey'),dict(points=[[0,-2.5],[0,2.5]],color='black'),dict(points=[[-u,0],[-u,h]],arrow=True,color='tab:green'),dict(points=[[-u,h],[0,h]],arrow=True)]
 end=max(v,4) if v>0 else 4
 seg.extend([dict(points=[[0,h],[end,h*(1-end/f)]],arrow=True),dict(points=[[-u,h],[end,-end*h/u]],arrow=True)])
 if v<0:seg.extend([dict(points=[[0,h],[v,hi]],style='--',color='tab:orange'),dict(points=[[0,0],[v,hi]],style='--',color='tab:orange')])
 seg.append(dict(points=[[v,0],[v,hi]],arrow=True,color='tab:red'))
 A.geometry(key,seg,labels=[[.15,2.5,'Lens'],[-2,-.35,'F'],[2,-.35,'F'],[-u-.5,h+.2,'Object'],[v-.5,hi+(.2 if hi>0 else -.35),'Image']],title=title)
lensdiagram('4.3.3-12-M01',6,3,1,'Real image: converging lens, f = 2 cm')
add('4.3.3-12-M02','An object inside a converging lens’s focal length forms an upright enlarged virtual image in the thin-lens model.','The image lies on the object side, where backward extensions of outgoing rays meet.','For focal length 2 cm and a 1 cm tall object 1 cm from the lens, the construction gives a 2 cm tall virtual image 2 cm on the object side. Use dashed extensions: actual rays do not travel backward to that point after leaving the lens. This is the basic magnifying-glass arrangement.','Draw the image of a 1 cm tall object 1 cm before a converging lens with focal length 2 cm.','Draw a parallel ray refracted toward the far focus and an undeviated centre ray.~Extend the diverging outgoing rays backward to a virtual upright image 2 cm before the lens and 2 cm tall.')
lensdiagram('4.3.3-12-M02',1,-2,1,'Virtual image: converging lens, f = 2 cm')
add('4.3.3-13','A real image is formed by actual convergence of light rays.','A virtual image is located by backward extensions rather than actual convergence at that location.','A real image can be received on a screen placed at its image location. A virtual image can be seen by an eye receiving the diverging rays, but cannot be collected on a screen placed at the apparent location. Visibility alone does not establish that an image is real.','Explain the distinction between real and virtual images.','Actual rays converge at a real image.~At a virtual image location, backward extensions meet while actual rays do not.')
add('4.3.3-14','A converging lens’s image characteristics depend on object distance relative to focal length.','Beyond 2f the real image is reduced; at 2f equal-sized; between f and 2f enlarged; inside f the image is virtual and enlarged.','Real images in these basic cases are inverted. The inside-f virtual image is upright. At the focal position, ideal outgoing rays are parallel and no finite image position is obtained on a nearby screen. State the object condition before predicting image size or nature.','State the image characteristics for an object beyond twice the focal length of a converging lens.','The image is real, inverted and reduced.')
add('4.3.3-15','A camera and eye use converging optics to form real inverted images on receiving surfaces.','A camera sensor or film corresponds to the retina as the image-receiving surface.','Use the real-image ray construction with a receiving surface at the convergence point. For the same simplified geometry, the surface is 3 cm behind the lens in the 6 cm object, 2 cm focal-length model. The eye changes optical power by accommodation; a simple camera can instead change lens-to-sensor separation. Aperture control regulates admitted light, not the fundamental real-image criterion.','Explain using a ray diagram how a camera and eye form images on their receiving surfaces.','Link actual ray convergence to a real inverted image on sensor or retina.~Both use converging optics, while focusing mechanisms differ.')
lensdiagram('4.3.3-15',6,3,1,'Camera/eye model: receiving surface at real image')
add('4.3.3-15-M01','A simple camera forms a real inverted image on film or an electronic sensor.','Focusing places the image plane at that receiving surface.','For a nearby object, the required lens-to-sensor separation differs from the distant-object setting. A simple camera moves the lens relative to the fixed sensor. The aperture controls light admission; a shutter controls exposure time. These roles should not be confused with focusing.','Describe focusing in a simple camera using a ray diagram with the sensor at the image plane.','Adjust the lens position relative to the sensor until a sharp real image falls on it.')
add('4.3.3-15-M02','The eye forms a real inverted image on the retina.','Accommodation changes the shape and optical power of the eye’s lens.','The cornea also contributes refraction, but the simplified syllabus comparison emphasises the lens and retina. The lens becomes more strongly converging for nearer objects in normal accommodation. The retina detects the image; the brain’s interpretation does not make the optical image upright on the retina.','Describe using a ray diagram how the eye forms an image on the retina after focusing on a nearer object.','Accommodation changes the lens shape to increase its converging power while the image remains on the retina.')
add('4.3.3-15-M03','A simple camera can focus by moving its lens relative to its receiving surface.','The eye mainly accommodates by changing lens power while retina position is approximately fixed.','Both require actual ray convergence at the receiving surface. Compare the changed feature rather than merely saying each has a lens. More complex cameras can have internal moving groups, but the elementary comparison concerns the simple model.','Describe the focusing difference between a simple camera and the human eye using a labelled ray diagram to locate the receiving surface.','A simple camera changes lens-to-sensor separation, whereas the eye changes lens power by accommodation.')
add('4.3.3-16','Short sight and long sight are different failures to focus an intended object on the retina.','The corrective lens type depends on whether the uncorrected convergence is too early or too late.','Short sight commonly blurs distant objects because their image would lie in front of the retina; a diverging correction reduces convergence. Long sight can blur near objects because the required image would lie behind the retina; a converging correction adds convergence. This is an educational optical model rather than an individual prescription.','Explain why short sight and long sight require different corrective lens types.','Link short sight’s premature convergence to a diverging correction.~Link long sight’s insufficient near-object convergence to a converging correction.')
add('4.3.3-16-M01','In short sight, distant-object rays would focus in front of the retina without correction.','A diverging corrective lens can reduce the incoming bundle’s convergence.','The correction lets the eye form its image farther back on the retina. Keep the ray account qualitative here; a separate treatment of diverging-lens formulae and image calculations is outside this topic’s intended depth. The defect concerns focus location, not a claim that the retina emits light.','Explain how a diverging lens corrects short sight.','The uncorrected distant-object rays converge in front of the retina.~A diverging lens reduces their convergence so the eye can focus them on the retina.')
add('4.3.3-16-M02','In long sight, near-object rays can be insufficiently converged to focus on the retina.','A converging corrective lens supplies additional convergence.','Without suitable accommodation or correction, the ideal focus for those near rays would be behind the retina. The corrective lens shifts the focus toward the retinal surface. Describe the near-object condition; the optical defect is not simply inability to see any distant object.','Explain how a converging lens can correct long sight for nearby objects.','The uncorrected rays would focus behind the retina.~The corrective lens adds convergence so the image can form on the retina.')
add('4.3.3-17','Refraction explains several natural apparent-position and colour effects.','Dispersion, internal reflection and changing air index add specific features to different examples.','A prism separates white light because its index depends on wavelength. Water droplets combine refraction and internal reflection to form a rainbow. A submerged object can appear shallower because emerging rays bend, while a mirage involves direction changes through air layers of different refractive index. Use the mechanism appropriate to each case rather than attributing all to reflection alone.','Explain the common optical link between a rainbow and an apparently shallow submerged object.','Link both to refraction at boundaries that changes ray directions.~A rainbow additionally needs dispersion and internal reflection in droplets, while apparent depth follows backward extension of emerging rays.')
add('4.3.3-17-M01','Dispersion separates white light because a material’s refractive index varies with wavelength.','The visible sequence from red to violet is red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet.','In the usual glass-prism case, violet deviates more than red. White light already contains the different colour components; the prism separates their directions rather than manufacturing the colours. A narrow incident slit makes the separated spectrum easier to distinguish on a screen.','Explain how a glass prism produces a spectrum from white light.','Its refractive index depends on wavelength, so the colours refract by different amounts.~They emerge in separated directions, with violet more deviated than red in the usual glass prism.')
add('4.3.3-17-M02','A primary rainbow involves refraction into droplets, one internal reflection and refraction out.','Dispersion directs different colours toward the observer at different angles.','Many droplets at suitable viewing directions contribute light. The observer normally has the Sun behind and droplets ahead. Red appears on the outer edge and violet on the inner edge of the primary bow. The internally reflected part need not be described as total internal reflection for the usual primary-rainbow path.','Explain the main light-path stages in a primary rainbow.','Sunlight refracts and disperses on entering droplets, then undergoes internal reflection.~It refracts out again, directing different colours toward the observer at different angles.')
add('4.3.3-17-M03','Light from an underwater object bends away from the normal on entering air.','An observer traces the emerging directions backward and sees an apparent position closer to the surface in the usual near-normal view.','The apparent depth differs from actual depth. Refraction can also change apparent size because ray directions and angular extent change, but the result depends on viewing geometry. Do not use a blanket claim that every underwater object is enlarged by one fixed factor. A ray sketch should show dashed backward extensions to the apparent point.','Explain why a submerged coin appears shallower when viewed from above.','Its rays bend away from the normal on leaving water for air.~Their backward extensions meet nearer the surface than the real coin.')
add('4.3.3-17-M04','A mirage can result when air temperature layers create a refractive-index gradient.','Rays can curve through successive small refractions and reach the eye from an unexpected direction.','Above a hot road, lower warmer air commonly has a lower index. A ray from the sky can bend upward through this gradient and enter an observer’s eye, giving an apparent sky-like patch on the road. The layered model can include limiting internal-reflection behaviour; a literal pool of water is not needed.','Explain qualitatively why a hot-road mirage can resemble water without water being present.','Different air layers change the light path through refraction.~Sky light reaches the eye from a direction that is interpreted as a lower reflected image, producing the apparent patch.')
A.misconception('MIS-12','Reflection returns light into the incident region, while refraction concerns transmission with changed propagation in another medium.','Both processes can occur at the same interface.','At an air-glass boundary, some light can reflect in air and some can enter glass with changed speed. Calling the transmitted bent ray reflected mixes up the side of the interface on which it propagates. Track the ray and the medium instead of naming any direction change reflection.','Light crosses from air into glass and bends. Explain why naming that transmitted ray reflected is incorrect.','The error confuses return into the original medium with entry into another.~The ray is refracted because it enters glass with changed propagation.')
A.misconception('MIS-13','An image can be visible even if no actual rays meet at its apparent position.','Virtual images are located by extensions, whereas real images use actual convergence.','A magnifying-glass virtual image can be seen through the lens, but a screen placed at its apparent location does not collect it. Visibility means rays enter the eye; it does not mean those rays originated or met at the apparent point.','A learner calls a magnifying glass’s upright virtual image real because it is visible. Explain the error.','Visibility does not establish actual convergence at the image location.~The correct distinction is that backward extensions meet there, so the image is virtual.')
A.misconception('MIS-14','Optical incidence and refraction angles use the normal, not the surface.','A stated surface angle must be complemented to 90° before use as a normal angle.','A transmitted ray 60° to a flat surface is 30° to its normal. Substituting 60° as the refraction angle into the index relation changes the sine ratio. A diagram with its normal drawn explicitly prevents mixing the two references.','A transmitted ray is 60° to the surface. Explain why r = 60° is incorrect and give the correct r.','The error uses the surface rather than its perpendicular normal.~The correct refraction angle is 90° − 60° = 30°.')
for key in ['4.3.3-12','4.3.3-15-M01','4.3.3-15-M02','4.3.3-15-M03']:
 lensdiagram(key,6,3,1,'Converging optics: receiving surface at actual ray meeting')
C=A.choice
C('4.3.3-02','At normal incidence into glass, what can change?',('Speed and wavelength','Frequency remains fixed and no bending is required.'),('Frequency because the source changes','The source has not changed.'),('Direction must turn by 90°','There is no bending at normal incidence.'),('The light becomes sound','The wave type remains electromagnetic.'))
C('4.3.3-06','Critical angle concerns which direction?',('Higher index toward lower index','That permits the limiting 90° refracted ray.'),('Air toward higher-index glass','This is not the total-internal-reflection direction.'),('Any direction with zero incidence','The threshold is not normal incidence.'),('Only parallel mirrors','It is a refractive-interface concept.'))
C('4.3.3-13','What locates a virtual image?',('Backward extensions of outgoing rays','Actual rays do not meet there.'),('Actual convergence on a screen','That locates a real image.'),('Its visibility alone','Both image types can be seen.'),('The lens colour alone','That does not establish image nature.'))
C('4.3.3-16-M01','Which corrects short sight in the basic model?',('A diverging lens','It reduces premature convergence.'),('A converging lens','This generally adds convergence.'),('An opaque screen over the eye','That blocks useful light.'),('A flat coloured card','It does not provide the needed correction.'))
C('4.3.3-17-M01','Which colour deviates more in a usual glass prism?',('Violet','Its refractive index is larger than red’s in this case.'),('Red','Red is less deviated in this comparison.'),('All colours equally','That would give no dispersion.'),('No colour can emerge','A transmitted spectrum can form.'))
C('4.3.3-15-M02','Where does the eye form its optical image?',('Retina','This is its receiving surface.'),('Outside the pupil','The image forms inside the eye.'),('On the front of a corrective lens','That lens helps adjust convergence.'),('At a virtual point behind the head','This is not the normal retinal image.'))
C('4.3.3-11','Which distant object is suitable for a classroom focal-length estimate?',('A distant building','Its approximately parallel rays can form a safe screen image.'),('The Sun viewed through the lens','Concentrated sunlight presents a hazard.'),('A fingertip inside the focal distance','It does not approximate a distant source.'),('A mark on the lens surface','It is not a distant object.'))
C('4.3.3-05','What is n for sin i = 0.75 and sin r = 0.50?',('1.5','Divide the sines.'),('0.667','This reverses the ratio.'),('0.25','This subtracts them.'),('1.25','This adds them.'))
C('4.3.3-08','Vacuum speed is 3 × 10⁸ m/s and material speed 2.4 × 10⁸ m/s. What is n?',('1.25','Use vacuum speed over material speed.'),('0.8','This reverses the ratio.'),('5.4','Adding speeds does not give index.'),('0.6','A speed difference is not the index.'))
C('4.3.3-04','A ray is 25° to the surface. What is its normal angle?',('65°','Use the complementary angle.'),('25°','This uses the wrong reference.'),('50°','Doubling is not the conversion.'),('115°','The intended acute angle is 65°.'))
C('4.3.3-14','An object lies inside a converging lens’s focal length. What image results?',('Upright enlarged virtual','Outgoing rays diverge and extensions meet.'),('Reduced inverted real','That fits an object beyond 2f.'),('Equal-sized real','That fits the 2f case.'),('A real image on the opposite-side screen','Actual rays do not converge there.'))
C('4.3.3-07','Glass-to-air incidence exceeds its critical angle. What occurs in the ideal model?',('Total internal reflection','The transmission condition is exceeded.'),('Refraction at 90° exactly','That occurs at the critical angle, not above.'),('No interaction with the boundary','The light is reflected.'),('Frequency doubles','Reflection does not require that frequency change.'))
P=A.part
P('4.3.3-10','State the name of the point where axis-parallel rays meet after a converging lens.','Principal focus.',1,'One mark for principal focus.')
P('4.3.3-13','State whether an image formed by actual ray convergence is real or virtual.','Real.',1,'One mark for real.')
P('4.3.3-07','Explain the conditions for total internal reflection.','Light travels from higher to lower index and its internal incidence exceeds the critical angle.',2,'One mark for index direction; one for angle above critical.')
P('4.3.3-05','Calculate n for sin i = 0.66 and sin r = 0.44.','n = 0.66/0.44 = 1.5.',2,'One mark for sine ratio; one for 1.5.')
P('4.3.3-08','Calculate light speed in a material of n = 1.5 when vacuum speed is 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s.','v = c0/n = 2.0 × 10⁸ m/s.',2,'One mark for dividing vacuum speed by index; one for 2.0 × 10⁸ m/s.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot sin i vertically against sin r horizontally using angles measured from the entry normal.',unit='dimensionless',meaning='The sin i against sin r gradient is the air-to-block refractive index. Reversing the axes would give its reciprocal.',method=['Trace a rectangular transparent block and mark a chosen entry point.','Direct a narrow ray at that point and mark incident and emergent paths.','Remove the block, join internal entry and exit points and construct the entry normal.','Measure i and r from that normal and calculate their sines in degree mode.','Repeat for several incidence angles and repeat alignments while using the same block.'],control='Keep the same wavelength or source and block, use thin lines, separate marking points well and avoid eye exposure to intense beams.',table='Record i / degree, r / degree, sin i and sin r, with the sine columns unitless.',conclusion='Fit sin i against sin r; a straight near-origin trend supports the relation and its slope estimates the relative refractive index.')
